Using a AWS Instance as VPN using WireGuard

1. Setup an AWS Instance with UDP port 51820 (WireGuard) and SSH port 22 Open in Security Group
2. 
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y wireguard
# Server keys
wg genkey | sudo tee /etc/wireguard/server_private.key | wg pubkey | sudo tee /etc/wireguard/server_public.key

# Client keys (for your Mac)
wg genkey | tee ~/client_private.key | wg pubkey | tee ~/client_public.key

# View all 4 keys — copy these somewhere
sudo cat /etc/wireguard/server_private.key
sudo cat /etc/wireguard/server_public.key
cat ~/client_private.key
cat ~/client_public.key

3. 
echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
sudo sysctl -p

4.
ip route | grep default
(Look for the interface name — on Amazon Linux it's usually eth0 or ens5. Note it down, you'll need it in the next step.)

5.
sudo vi /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf

6. 
[Interface]
Address = 10.0.0.1/24
ListenPort = 51820
PrivateKey = <contents of server_private.key>

PostUp = iptables -A FORWARD -i wg0 -j ACCEPT; iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o ens5 -j MASQUERADE
PostDown = iptables -D FORWARD -i wg0 -j ACCEPT; iptables -t nat -D POSTROUTING -o ens5 -j MASQUERADE

[Peer]
PublicKey = <contents of client_public.key>
AllowedIPs = 10.0.0.2/32

7. Start WireGuard
sudo dnf install -y iptables
sudo systemctl enable wg-quick@wg0
sudo systemctl start wg-quick@wg0

# Verify it's running
sudo wg show

8.
In Laptop install WireGuard from appStore

9. Create ~/wg0.conf
[Interface]
Address = 10.0.0.2/24
PrivateKey = <client_private_key>
DNS = 1.1.1.1

[Peer]
PublicKey = <server_public_key>
Endpoint = <EC2_ELASTIC_IP>:51820
AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0    # Routes ALL traffic through the tunnel
PersistentKeepalive = 25

9. Import and connect from app UI
From Terminal

# To connect:
sudo wg-quick up ~/wg0.conf

# To disconnect:
sudo wg-quick down ~/wg0.conf
